{"code":"\"\"\"Error types for minidb.\n\nThis module holds the single public exception of the package. It depends on\nnothing else, so every other module may import it without creating a cycle.\n\"\"\"\n\n__all__ = [\"QueryError\"]\n\n\nclass QueryError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Raised for any query that minidb cannot execute.\n\n    This covers malformed SQL, unknown tables or columns, ambiguous column\n    references, mixed-type comparisons, negative LIMIT/OFFSET values and type\n    errors. It is the only exception the public API is allowed to raise, so\n    callers branch on the exception type rather than on the message text.\n    \"\"\"\n","concerns":["The peer review raised no defect in errors.py; every finding concerns parser.py, planner.py or engine.py, so this file is unchanged from round 1 and still exports QueryError and nothing else, as published.","Several reported failures (parser importing a non-existent `Expr`, planner calling `ref.ref_name()`, planner reading `select.from_table` instead of `select.from_`, planner indexing a non-dict `tables`) would surface as ImportError, AttributeError or TypeError. Those cannot be fixed from errors.py: QueryError is a plain Exception with no hooks, so api.query's `except Exception -> raise QueryError(...) from exc` remains the only place that converts them, and an ImportError at package import time happens before any conversion is possible.","No error-code enum, factory helper or subclass hierarchy is added, since the published interface promises exactly one plain exception class and dependent modules were written against `raise QueryError(<message>)`."],"exports":["QueryError"],"path":"minidb/errors.py"}